Home › Blog › Online Business › A Guide to Content Delivery Network: What Is a CDN, How It Works and How It Helps Your Website
Several leading sites have already started using a CDN. According to BuiltWith, more than 66% of the top 10,000 websites are using a content delivery network.
Let’s find out more about CDN.
A content delivery network is a network of servers that deliver web pages and other content to users based on their geographical locations. In other words, it helps reduce the time taken by the remote server to respond back with the data requested by the end-users.
The primary server hosting this site is in London, UK. Owing to the vast physical distance, it will take considerable time for the website to load.
If you request the same content again, one of those edge servers will deliver it to you directly, reducing the loading time further. A CDN can store different types of content including images, CSS, JavaScript, HTML along with 4K and HD videos.
Some of the web hosting service providers charge based on the bandwidth. If you are using this type of service, using a CDN can reduce your hosting costs.
In other words, the text on your site can’t wait until the images load correctly. Poor implementation of the CDN often causes this problem.
However, the trend is gradually shifting towards using more than one CDN providers. Multiple CDNs come with plenty of benefits such as comprehensive coverage, improved loading speed, reduced downtime due to single provider outage and cheaper hosting costs.
The DNS recognizes the hostnames of a CDN provider, which in turn, identifies the IP address of the web servers nearest to your users. Thus, you have the freedom to diversify your services.
However, this option is also costlier compared to automated CDN management as you will have to pay each CDN provider separately. Similarly, you will need to configure and optimize each CDN separately as well, turning this method into a labor-intensive and time-consuming option.
As you know, the loading speed is one of the most critical factors for improving your site’s SEO. However, CDN can boost the SEO in other ways as well.
Increased page loading speed leads to better content delivery, which in turn, enhances the user experience. It is perhaps the most crucial SEO factor that Google uses to determine the search engine ranking of your site.
Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) or Transport Layer Security (TLS) protection plays a critical role in enhancing and maintaining your SERP ranking. The CDN allows you to run your website on secure servers even though the main site isn’t secure.
However, using a CDN also comes with a few risks that you can mitigate with proper configuration. That’s why you need to know about what a CDN is, how it works, and how it can improve your site’s SEO.
Jeff Starr (blog at Perishable Press, Twitter @Perishable) is the go-to guy when you have a problem with your WordPress blog. An editor, a book author, a web developer, and a well-recognized WordPress guru – Jeff has influenced and helped thousands, if not more, of bloggers and web developers with his writing.
Q1: Hi Jeff, thanks a lot for being with us today. Let's start with some introduction!
Q3: Great, it's good to know that you are satisfied with your current web host. What makes you chose Media Temple at the first place?
The servers were inconsistent and the support staff (with an exception or two) was pretty horrible, so I finally got fed up and decided to find something better. After much research I finally chose Media Temple because of their reported 1) consistency/uptime, 2) excellent customer service, 3) not too crazy expensive pricing.
Note: Media Temple was acquired by GoDaddy in October 2013. Review and compare Media Temple with other web hosts in our web hosting review index.
Well the thing to keep in mind is that Perishable Press is just one of my 12 or so websites.. they all reside on the same server, and collectively consume a LOT of resources.
Q5: Jeff, security is always a major concern to many WordPress users – myself included. Without sharing too many secrets, can you tell us what are you doing to protect Perishable Press from hackers and spammers?
Stay current, stay informed, don't get lazy, don't assume anything, double-check everything, don't use pirated plugins/themes/scripts/etc., keep an eye on server logs, and scan/monitor everything.
