Blogging is the act of putting your thoughts online for other people to read. That act itself is not difficult, however in order to blog effectively, we rely on many different tools.
Today I would like to share with you the 10 blogging tools I cannot do without. These resources are not necessarily the best; they are simply the ones I have become accustomed to using for certain tasks.
I have used it for years to check the latest articles on important news-breaking websites. The upcoming closure of Google Reader on July 1 2013 has seen many more people turn to NetVibes to stay up to date with content websites.
There are dozens of great note-taking applications available online. I just love the simplicity of Google Docs; A blank document is all I need.
I use FileZilla to upload all core fires, banner images, themes, plugins, and more. Sure, you can manage your blog using a file manager, however the process is slow and clunky.
When I first started blogging in 2006 I tried several popular blogging platforms. WordPress stood against the competition due to the number of themes and plugins it had available to it.
VaultPress makes this process simple by backing up your blog or website every single hour. The service was developed by Automattic, the company who develops WordPress.
Google Alerts allows you to get notifications about anything. I use it to get notified of any reviews of my books and any links to my blog.
Out of all the services listed in this article, Google Alerts is the one that I do not rely on every day.
I use DropBox to backup all important website files including files, themes, plugins, logos, notes, and more. All of my files are automatically synced across all my computers and I can access important files from any device.
Blog posts without images look dull and get shared less often on social media websites. Therefore, I take screenshots every day for my blog posts.
For years I used Photoshop to edit images, however over the last few years, I moved towards free photo editing solutions. GIMP was the obvious choice but the transition to Gimphoto was easier as the menu interface was based on Photoshop's.
