Why should you want to try and sell an online course on a mostly unknown topic?
But you do for a reason:
And that's great!
It only requires more work, because it's hard to convince people to follow anything on a topic that is hardly known or less than popular, let alone buy it.
There's a strategy that I followed and I am following, and other people followed as well. With this post, I'm going to guide you from sparking interest in a non-popular topic or niche, to making money and generating traffic with your online course.
Sell your special e-course at a smaller price than your regular course or other products
Make it a workshop before you upgrade to an online course
The reality is that you can only sell an online course on a mostly unknown topic to an audience who already knows and trusts you!
The very first step to sell your special online course is to start with the people who know and trust you – your subscribers, blog readers, engaged social followers, and current students (if you run other courses).
Start with your most trafficked post and add a paragraph with CTA about your new idea, directing them to a landing page where you explain your idea in detail and why you'd love to teach it to others.
Then you can write a new blog post introducing the idea to newbies, link it to the landing page where you explain what you want to teach your readers about it, and link this blog post from older posts that are closely related.
You could introduce them to your little-known niche with a “P.S.” at the end of your new emails, or you can introduce them to the topic with a series of emails, at the end of which you'll ask for feedback, such like: “Would you like to signup for an early bird course in (Topic Name)?”
Ask them about their interest in your unpopular niche: Are they curious?
What if you offered a free trial of your online course for them to learn more?
4. With Current Students
Add a paragraph or two about it in the newsletter of your existing course, or give your students an extra lecture where you introduce your topic and some applications.
Invite each category of trusting users shown in the previous point to participate in surveys about the new course.
Your target audience doesn't know yet whether they'll be interested in what your course has to offer, or if that more obscure topic you seldom blog about is worth their time and money.
You can even make a public survey or poll as a response to a video of yourself introducing the topic, that you might also want to help go viral with shareable buttons.
Would you be following me for this new project?
If you could create a project around this idea, what could it be?
Example: I have a poll widget on my character blogging site (see screenshot above) asking visitors “What's your biggest question or problem with Character Blogging?
2. Make the poll useful to build the course, because you want it to tell you what people really want to know about this topic and the problems they might have, doubts, and any wrong opinions about it that you can correct.
Promote The E-Course Launch on Your Channels Promotion must be handled with care because you are promoting a niche or a topic that probably doesn't ring an immediate bell with your audience, so you have to lay the foundations of that knowledge first.
You need this laying ground to launch your new course about such a little-known or unpopular niche.
I'm a member of the MyBlogU community and there I created a brainstorming project to ask people what they would like to see in a course on character blogging for business, a concept I already introduced to the community:
You can also offer blog posts or free content (e.g. short ebooks) to the community to learn more about your idea, and how the course will take this idea and shape it into teachable units, and what people can expect to learn from your modules.
You may also create a webinar with a Q&A at the end and invite your subscribers to join it.
This special non-popular niche you are building an online course on is something you most likely already covered in your blog, so you might want to link any promotional post you write now to this older content and start teaching right there, on your blog!
Tell your friends about your new idea and ask them to let you guest post on their blog to introduce your little-known niche to their audience and invite them to enroll when you launch the course.
Highlight the benefits your students will get by joining the course upon launch, and how it can effectively integrate their current learning.
Your existing channels are only the beginning of your promotional efforts.
The good news is that once you've done a good job on your existing channels, you will have built a layer of authority that will help you get other opportunities more easily.
Respond to media queries about some related topic and use the opportunity to introduce the new idea. For example, if a blogger is looking for out-of-the-box blogging ideas, I can tell them about my business character blogging project and the course I'm making to teach others how to character blog for money.
Search for blogs in niches that are closely related to yours or at least in the niche of your blog, and use the persuasive power of email outreach to pitch them a blog post about the subject of your course.
While any backlinks to your blog or course landing page will most likely end up in the bio field, you can create awareness in the post and link that awareness to your name. 3.
Since there's pretty much no market for character blogging for business, and some of what's online about it were my own writing, the course instructor told me I had to create interest first.
So far I managed to build a small community around my character blogging blog and I already spread some word about the course I'm building for those who are starting to get interested.
Many Facebook groups for blogger growth allow co-promotion. Unless it's a group that you founded or you have special privileges to post promo content, you have to wait for weekly or monthly threads to promote a specific blog post or landing page.
You might get some of the group members interested and potentially reach their networks.
You can create a Twitter chat about your idea and the course you want to launch to get people interested from Twitter.
I did a lot of ask me anything about SEO sessions in free, no-promo facebook groups, a lot of what you call today tap-on-the-shoulder marketing, where I had my Facebook profile optimized (branded graphics How and CTA to get on my list) and I would post or answer questions related to my topic in free groups so that when people would hover over my name, they'd get curious and sign up for my list.
Before she could successfully launch her girl empowerment digital course at Life Evolutions, Melody Pourmoradi created buzz for her idea writing guest posts and working with brands:
I have also worked with various brands as an influencer for spreading the message of girls empowerment by partnering with women who can teach and spread the GiRLiFE vision. Lastly, I have created a Facebook group all about girls empowerment where I share as much valuable content as I can while creating buzz for the e-course.
Your sales page exists for one and one reason only:
But if you want to explain why and how this course is beneficial to your audience, you must explain in as much detail as possible the practical benefits for taking this course and how the course will be taught.
While your sales page is not there to teach, you have to show suspicious prospective students that your unknown or non-popular niche rocks and can change their life, and it isn't less valuable only because nobody talks about it.
People need to see this content before they enroll.
The first part is for those who need less information to be persuaded to signup
A third layer is for those who need even more information, and you'll want to add extra content exactly for this group of people.
6. Sell Your Special Course at a Smaller Price than Your Regular Course or Other Products
Offer a risk-free 30-day trial of the course that doesn't require a credit card
Offer a good discount (at least 15%) to anyone who joins the course after taking action (become a subscriber, comment on any number of posts, etc.)
When you have the early bird or free trial version of your course ready, encourage and collect testimonials from the students who are trying out your course for the first time: This feedback will turn out very useful to make the sales page more effective as well as your blog posts and any free content you use to make a sale.
BONUS: Make It a Workshop Before Upgrading It to a Full Course
A workshop is more hands-on than a regular online course, and people can immediately put what they learn into practice. In her post on how to monetize your site by creating your first online workshop, Lori Soard explains that people love to act on what they learn, so when you approach your audience with something less than popular, you'll want to earn their favor with something actionable they can put to use right away!
Make this a one-week workshop that teaches people by example. For me, the workshop I'm working on is on teaching people how to setup a character blog and plan a character's blog posts for money.
Once you have the plan set up for your special course, it's time to create the learning modules and look for the platforms that will allow you to distribute it to your students. 1.
Pick a piece of paper or grab your digital drawing tool and begin to map out the way you want to present the material to your students.
2. Host It for Free/Paid or Run It Yourself
Here I'm going to addGoogle Classroom, a platform by Google allows you to create a virtual classroom for free. You can upload assignments, videos, study materials and ask questions to the class.
Along with Moodle, there are other two good softwares to self-host your course:
Claroline – A user-friendly and intuitive platform to install and run on your website for your courses. I've been using this one for years to host self-learning modules for my lifelong learning goals and it works like a charm.
Use Live Streaming Facebook Live gives you the option to create live streaming sessions that you can use with your students in groups you created just for your course or on the course Page.
Generating Traffic for Your Blog
Most likely, you have written at least a few times about this non-popular topic, but chances are your posts didn't receive the attention you hoped for.
Include your blog posts among the recommended or required readings for your students: That will force your students to continuously go back to your blog to read the material, and you can strategically add CTAs that will improve the overall interest in your blog content and viral traffic (e.g. encourage your students to share specific pieces of content if they want their friends to learn about the subject they're studying)
All the buzz work and link building you have done to promote your online course already generated plenty of traffic to specific pages of your blog and landing pages – keep doing that!
Being original may require more legwork than running with popular topics, and you may have to rely less on organic keywords if there's little to no demand for your unique course on such a new or unknown topic, but this is not an adventure for the brave — it's entirely doable.
Allow people to give it a try for free, let them experiment with the unknown that you are offering. It might become known tomorrow.
Home › Blog › Blogging Tips › 6 Tips to Use Content Marketing to Double Your Site Traffic
In some ways, content marketing predates the Internet. To summarize, content marketing is the use of content (text, photos, videos and perhaps even audio) to build interest among your target audience who may then be lured in to buy your product.
In this article, however, we will restrict ourselves to the conventional definition – that is, the use of text, videos, images and audio over online platforms to build traffic, generate leads and eventually convert customers.
This will give you an idea of the kind of content that will appeal to these users. For instance, if you target teenagers who hang out on social networking platforms like Snapchat, then perhaps it is a good idea to make use of trendy content formats like memes (yes, memes!)
Second, generate link-baiting content whose purpose is to build new inbound links to your website. These could be viral images or exhaustive stat pieces that other blogs like linking to as part of their content.
This three-pronged strategy is quite useful because it helps you build an authoritative site that Google trusts. Once this is achieved, your SEO-focused content starts ranking on top of searches and brings in a lot of targeted visitors.
The idea of content marketing is to establish sections on your website that appeal in one way or another to your target audience. It does not always have to be self-generated content.
An example of script-based content generation is Dove’s Speak Beautiful campaign. For this, the soap brand partnered with Twitter to build a microsite that allowed users to measure the positivity or negativity in their tweets.
Content marketing is a momentum-based strategy where the uptick may be slow and gradual to begin with, but can grow exponentially over time. The momentum is often higher if you provide your content a helpful nudge in the early stages.
The strategy here is quite simple – identify the audience or group that will find your content appealing and spend a few marketing dollars reaching this audience with your content. This establishes virality that can gather momentum and help maximize your content reach.
Virality Getting your content to go viral is an extension of the momentum-based marketing strategy we discussed in the previous section.
Marketers realize the importance of quality over quantity when it comes to content marketing. One by-product of this realization is that long-form content is often regarded as higher quality and more important than short-form content.
Over the long-term, this strategy helps you build authority among your target audience and helps you convert customers. In addition to this, users who visit such threads also come across your submissions and are driven to your sales pages from there.
