Home › Blog › Blogging Tips › Which Email Newsletter Service Is Best for Your Blog?
No matter what your reasons for starting a blog, getting readers to read a post is just the first step. It’s only by developing your relationship with your readers that you’ll achieve your goals.
You understand the “why” behind email newsletters – but what about the “how”?
Founded in the 90s, Constant Contact has been around for a while. Known for their dedicated customer support, they focus on providing email newsletter service for small businesses and nonprofits.
Small businesses and nonprofits are their main target audience. If you’re not very tech-savvy and phone support is a priority for you, then Constant Contact may be a good choice.
There’s much more to MailChimp than their cute mascot, Freddy. They’re one of the most popular email newsletter providers for a reason.
Intuitive interface that’s easy for beginners to learn quickly
AWeber is one of the most established and well-known email newsletter providers.
Segmentation is awkwardly implemented. Subscribers are counted multiple times if they’re on multiple lists – which can drive your pricing up.
AWeber is geared more towards businesses or career email marketers than individuals. Honestly, AWeber offers some good features, but if you’re an individual blogger it’s nothing you can’t get in a better platform for the same price or cheaper.
Founded in 2013, ConvertKit is the new kid on the block, but they’ve certainly made a splash so far and are still growing rapidly. The founder is a professional blogger who also creates and sells his own products, and he created ConvertKit for a similar audience.
Convertkit ROCKS if you have multiple funnels running at the same time or you have multiple ways someone can enter your email list. The use of Triggers and Tags in Convertkit it super easy to help your customers navigate through the customer journey without mistakes of a client receiving an email that is intended for a prospect.
The email editor is very limited: you can’t customize font/color/size on emails without using code
ConvertKit is specifically designed for bloggers and digital marketers who want to sell their own products and services. If you want to run an email course, set up complicated funnels or audience segmentation, etc., then ConvertKit is for you.
Omnisend leverages on your social channels to do more than sending the newsletter.
Visual builder for all marketing aspects with professional templates to help you get started
This platform is for anyone who sells online and needs to build a better relationship with their customers. If it’s time to scale up from simple newsletter to omnichannel marketing automation, Omnisend is the perfect choice for you.
All of the above providers are good, and many of them provide a free trial or even a free level of service.
