Home › Blog › Blogging Tips › Is There Really Money to be Earned from Blogging?
When blogging first emerged on the scene, it was purely a means of personal expression. It allowed individuals to publish their personal thoughts, opinions, ideas about a particular topic and share them with readers, if they wanted to.
The question they must be asking themselves is “Can you actually make money from blogging?”
Every Tom, Dick and Harry seems to be blogging these days; many people see this activity as a way of earning money quickly, without a lot of effort.
Blogging involves doing the hard yards. It requires the highest levels of commitment from you that sees you publishing new content on your blog regularly.
Your content needs to stand out from the crowd and your style must be your own. If you do not present any new ideas on your blog in a way that is markedly different from your competition, why would people come to your blog?
If your blog isn’t earning money, take a very close look at it and check if it is saying the same thing that most other sites in your niche are saying. If it is, you’ve got a reason why your blog is not earning money.
A blog becomes profitable on if it is able to attract a large amount of readers. That’s a given, but you also need to engage with them on a one-on-one basis to ensure they keep coming back to your site and interacting with its content.
I am sure you’ve come across plenty of blogs where Ads stare at you from all corners of the page. There are different CTAs vying for your attention and content takes a back seat.
When a visitor lands on your blog, it mustn’t scream “I want money from my blog” at them; what it needs to convey is “I want to offer you the best information possible”. This is what works.
To put it simply, you can only earn money from your blog, if you’ve the right attitude. If you’ve dreams of becoming a millionaire in double quick time with your blogging efforts, that’s not going to happen.
Case Studies of Successful Golfing Bloggers
Tony Korologos, also known as the Media Guru behind Hooked on Golf Blog, started blogging in 2004 because he had a true passion for the game. His best friend suggested he blog about it, and the rest, as they say, is history.
His top two tips? Choose a unique topic and develop a voice unlike anyone else's.
In his guide to starting a blog, Jerry Low shares a story about his first attempt to make money online. Although that attempt wasn't successful, he kept trying until he found the right niche and was able to find a topic that needed covered and to monetize it.
“Choosing golf was secondary at the time – a sport and a topic I knew about from my youth. The fact that it grew into something significant was definitely a surprise.”
Learning your craft is absolutely vital if you want to get a leg up on the competition. For example, one of the first things you'll want to master is how to capture and keep your readers' attention.
For one thing, there are a whole lot more blogs these days, so you need a unique niche or voice in order to stand out. My sense is that having a certain blogging personality and approach are what helps build a readership and why people keep coming back.
Just glancing at Neil's blog, there are several things that make it a blog readers will want to return to. His navigation is uncluttered and simple.
Finding Your Niche There are numerous golf blogs out there, so if you want to one, you need to:
Find your voice. How do you write differently from anyone else?
If you plan to write about golfing for kids, then look at what is being done out there on the blogs. What isn't covered and how can you cover it better?
Write a book and sell it. You can sell it on your blog and also sell on sites like Amazon with their self-publishing platforms.
Create a members only area with the really unique content. In order to access those items, readers will have to pay a small membership fee.
Accept private ads. Is there a local golf course that might want to advertise on your site?
Is a Golfing Blog for You?
