Idea Starters: 20 Phrases to Help You Come Up With Topics to Write About
The idea may come to you from any source, book, blog, magazine, or life event.
For it to go viral, your idea needs to be perfect (not just good) for your audience. ‘Perfect' here means it will resonate so strongly in the minds and hearts of your readers that they'll go I must share this RIGHT.
This is when your post goes viral – when it makes a difference. This guide is here to help you find out whether your blog idea has the potential to go viral and attract more readers and shares, plus a few tips for when you are about to hit the ‘Publish' button.
Your marketing data can help you decide whether this is a doable idea and something of interest to your audience.
For example, is there a topic that could start trending after a mention in a publication such as TechCrunch or Mashable?
Readers want to follow a blogger with a like mind. When you understand your audience well enough that you feel like your brains work on the same frequency, that's when your blog ideas will always resonate with your audience and… go viral.
Think about it: does your idea offer a solution to a problem that a good slice of your audience is facing?
Even Neil Patel wrote his post Why Do Sites Rank High on Google When They Aren’t Optimized?
Have you ever wondered why some sites rank high on Google when they aren’t optimized for search engines?
I’ve been asked this question a lot over the last few months, so I thought I would write a blog post explaining why that happens.
Before I write a blog post, I make sure that the topic is something people already talk about. You need to know your audience.
The more you know them well – and polls, surveys, Q&A, webinars and your list are all great ways to do this – the more you can respond to their needs with your blog posts. If it helps, think of yourself as a counselor and of your audience as your clients.
In other words, is your idea just opinion or can you support it with existing studies and reports?
While a research-based idea might work better for a niche or business blog, ideas based on opinion and feelings can still get traction if you back them up with research. Of course, a simple opinion piece might work but it will be difficult to make it go viral, because it will be like a personal blog entry – interesting, nice to read, but with little share value (unless you were Seth Godin).
Your take, research and opinion may not be enough to satiate your readers' hunger for knowledge.
Also, Lori Soard's post How to Find Interview Subjects and Conduct an Expert Interview for Your Blog is a great read to get started.
This is also a fantastic way to build relationships.
Your idea should answer a question, tackle a problem or offer a complete guide, and it better be comprehensive unless it's part of a series or you're trying to build a community and your posts act like discussion threads (that is what Jeff Goins does, but he also adds a comment that “it’s not the strong parts you leave out; it’s the weak ones”).
If you were them, what would you expect to read?
Feel they can trust the information you provide
A great post that goes viral has all these four ingredients, plus the “I've been looking for this for so long!
Do your beta readers feel your post is the guide to do something or the life-changing piece, one that can't be missed?
Is this post something you want to read on your blog (see #6 in the above list)?
Does it push you to do something about the topic at hand?
I noticed this happens a lot with my own posts: after some time, when I have a problem but I forgot the solution, I go back to the post I wrote and follow my own advice. When your idea works, and your post works, it works for you, too.
Are they approachable via email, contact form or social media?
Follow my email outreach guide for bloggers here at WHSR for templates and outreach ideas and techniques.
So far I have talked about the questions to ask yourself to see if your idea has the potential to go viral before you turn it into a blog post, and how to make sure the blog post itself lives up to that goal. But… what happens if you went through this guide and the result is that your idea doesn't have enough potential?
Come up with a new idea and start all over again
I tend to prefer the second. The truth is that every idea can be tweaked to make it reach virality potential:
You can narrow it if it's too broad
It resonates with the reader
You can support it with research
The topic has to be covered completely and deeply, not just touched upon with a light brush. In some instances, this aspect can also count for originality – the topic might have been covered before by hundreds of other bloggers, but never before in such depth.
