Alcohol is absolutely bad for the liver.
According to experts, although drinking is not a direct cause of liver cancer, it is a “catalyst” that promotes the occurrence and development of liver cancer.
The toxicity of alcohol on liver cells is mainly manifested by the excessive oxidation of lipid components on the surface of liver cell membranes by affecting liver metabolism, destroying the microtubules and mitochondria in liver cells, causing swelling and necrosis of liver cells, obstructing the decomposition and metabolism of fatty acids in liver, causing fat deposition in the liver, thereby forming fatty liver.
Drinking alcohol in moderation is good for your health, but drinking too much at a time and ingesting a lot of alcohol in a short period of time will also increase the oxygen consumption of the liver, resulting in acute alcoholism. “If you drink too much alcohol frequently for 5 consecutive years, you will have alcoholic fatty liver, alcoholic cirrhosis and even alcoholic hepatitis.” Therefore, experts say that you must control how much you drink.
